OOP Expert Checklist (Python / Backend)

A) Foundations & Modeling
- Object vs class vs instance
- Identity vs equality (is vs ==)
- State, behavior, invariants (protecting rules of the domain)
- Abstraction: hiding complexity, exposing stable contracts
- Encapsulation: protecting invariants (not “private variables”)
- Polymorphism: interchangeable behavior via common contract
- Inheritance: correct “is-a” only (avoid misuse)
- Composition over inheritance (default choice)
- Domain modeling: Entities, Value Objects, Aggregates, Services
- Choosing OOP vs functional vs plain data structures (tradeoffs)

B) Class Mechanics (Python-specific)
- Instance attributes vs class attributes
- Initialization lifecycle: __new__ vs __init__
- Attribute lookup order (instance dict → class dict → base classes/MRO)
- Method binding (bound methods) and how functions become methods
- Name mangling (__x) and visibility conventions (_x)
- @property / setters / invariants via properties
- __slots__ (memory/perf) + tradeoffs
- dataclasses: defaults, default_factory, __post_init__, frozen, slots

C) Inheritance, MRO, super()
- Method overriding rules and safe substitution
- MRO understanding and ability to predict resolution
- Cooperative multiple inheritance with super()
- Diamond problem awareness + safe patterns
- Mixins: when to use, how to keep them small and safe
- Avoiding fragile base class + deep inheritance chains

D) Interfaces & Contracts
- abc.ABC and @abstractmethod
- Interface-based design (program to abstractions)
- typing.Protocol for structural typing (where useful)
- Liskov Substitution Principle (subtypes must not break expectations)
- Interface segregation (small focused contracts)

E) SOLID (Practical Mastery)
- SRP: one reason to change; avoid God objects
- OCP: extend via new classes, not edits everywhere
- LSP: subtypes preserve behavior & expectations
- ISP: avoid “fat” interfaces; keep contracts narrow
- DIP: depend on abstractions; use dependency injection

F) Design Patterns (Know + Apply + Avoid Overuse)
Creational
- Factory / Abstract Factory
- Builder
- Prototype
- Singleton (know pitfalls; avoid global state)

Structural
- Adapter (wrapping 3rd-party APIs)
- Facade (simplifying a complex subsystem)
- Decorator (add behavior without modifying core)
- Proxy (caching, access control)
- Composite (tree structures)

Behavioral
- Strategy (pluggable algorithms / handlers)
- Observer (events/signals)
- Command (actions as objects)
- State (behavior changes with state)
- Template Method (shared skeleton, overridable steps)
- Chain of Responsibility (pipeline of handlers)

Architectural (OOP-friendly)
- Layered architecture
- Hexagonal / Ports & Adapters
- Repository + Unit of Work
- Domain Model vs Anemic Model (tradeoffs)

G) Dunder / Data Model Mastery (High Signal)
Representation
- __repr__, __str__

Equality & Ordering
- __eq__, __ne__, __lt__/__le__/__gt__/__ge__, total ordering
- __hash__ rules with mutability

Containers & Iteration
- __len__, __iter__, __next__
- __getitem__, __setitem__, __contains__

Callables & Context
- __call__
- __enter__, __exit__

Attribute Control
- __getattr__ vs __getattribute__
- __setattr__, __delattr__

Copying / Serialization (as needed)
- __copy__, __deepcopy__
- __getstate__, __setstate__ (optional)

H) Descriptors & Advanced Attribute Control
- Descriptor protocol: __get__, __set__, __delete__
- How @property is a descriptor
- Reusable validation/access descriptors (when justified)

I) Metaprogramming Relevant to OOP
- Class decorators
- __init_subclass__ (auto-registration / hooks)
- Metaclasses basics (type) + when to avoid
- Plugin/registry patterns (discoverability/extensibility)

J) Testability & Dependency Management
- Dependency injection (constructor / method)
- Mocking via interfaces/protocols
- Avoiding hard-coded globals/singletons
- Unit tests for behavior + invariants
- Designing for easy refactoring with tests

K) Robustness & Error Handling
- Custom exception hierarchies
- Fail-fast vs error accumulation
- Keeping invariants safe under exceptions (transaction-like behavior)
- Clear boundaries: where to catch vs where to raise

L) Concurrency + OOP (Real-world)
- Thread safety of mutable object state
- Immutability as a safety strategy
- Locks/queues around shared state (when needed)
- Designing objects safe for async workflows

M) Performance & Memory Awareness
- Object allocation overhead awareness
- __slots__ when justified
- Avoiding huge object graphs in hot paths
- Profiling OOP-heavy code (cProfile, memory tools)
- Serialization overhead awareness (DTOs vs domain objects)

N) Backend/Service OOP Design Skill (Interview gold)
- Thin controllers/handlers; logic in services/domain
- Repository pattern for DB interactions
- DTO/schema (Pydantic) separation from domain objects
- Strategy for partner/vendor integrations
- Event objects + handler polymorphism
- Idempotency and retries as explicit service behaviors

O) Refactoring & Code Smell Mastery (Expert Proof)
Identify smells
- God object, long method, shotgun surgery
- Feature envy, primitive obsession
- Tight coupling, deep inheritance, duplicated logic

Refactor safely
- Extract class/method
- Replace conditionals with polymorphism
- Introduce interface/abstraction
- Replace inheritance with composition
- Preserve behavior via tests while refactoring

P) Communication & Review Skills (Makes “expert” believable)
- Explain tradeoffs clearly (why this design)
- Predict change impact (extensibility)
- Do strong code reviews (actionable feedback)
- Design under constraints (time, cost, latency, maintainability)
- Share real project examples with measurable impact

Practical Interview “Expert Signals”
- Design a small system with clean classes in 20–30 minutes
- Spot SOLID violations and propose improvements quickly
- Explain MRO/super edge cases confidently
- Add new feature by adding classes (not editing many files)
- Tie OOP decisions to production concerns (testing, reliability, performance)